Franky Zapata arrived at this beach near  Calais hoping to make aviation history  normally super-confident he looked  nervous about the challenge ahead the 40  year old inventor knew the biggest  problem would be finding the ship  mid-channel  where he would have to refuel because  his kerosene powered device can only fly  for 10 minutes after much discussion and  preparation it was time for liftoff 26  miles of open sea stood between him and  history the launch itself went as  planned with hundreds of onlookers  coming out to see him off saboteur shot  to fame in France after his exploits at  the Bastille Day celebrations in Paris  on July the 14th the whole country has  got behind his invention but within  minutes of the launch it was clear that  something had gone badly wrong Frankie's  wife Kristin hugged their son Max as  news came through that he crashed into  the channel the refuelling stop had  proved to be his undoing these things  can happen and you can't foresee them he  travelled more kilometres than planned  he flew faster so there were good things  too the French government has given him  and million pounds of a grant to further  develop his fly board and he feels these  are early days for this device what  Frankie's teams say like all great  inventors he will learn from this and  come back even stronger yes it's a  setback but he wants to try again  exactly a hundred and ten years ago  Louie blury of blew off from northern  France to England making the first solo  flight across the channel Franky Zapata  is adamant he too can enter aviation  history but for now it's back to the  drawing board in the Brady Sky News in  sangat northern France
 

